fault), with early stop at a high threshold (\u00a75.3) and a documented reduced-budget fallback (\u00a76).\n- **Eval schedule:** every 500K env steps, 32 eval episodes under **deterministic/argmax policies** (for IQL: greedy, epsilon off). Final score = eval at the 10M budget (or at early stop).\n- **Random baseline:** 1000 random-action episodes per map on the same host, to estimate `WR_random` (see \u00a74).\n\n---\n\n## 4. (c) Metrics & normalization\n\n### 4.1 Primary metric \u2014 Normalized score (per map)\n`NormalizedScore = clip((WR_agent \u2212 WR_random) / (1 \u2212 WR_random), 0, 1)`\n- `WR_agent` = eval win rate over 32 deterministic-episode evals (win definition per SMAX: all enemies dead \u2192 win; all allies dead \u2192 loss; timeout \u2192 loss, per SMAX termination rules).\n- `WR_random` = measured on the same host from 1000 random-action episodes per map (\u00a73.4), not borrowed from literature. If `WR_random` \u2248 0, the formula reduces to `WR_agent`, which is reported un-normalized in the same table.\n- Because win rate is bounded, the normalization is affine and interpretable: it is the share of the random-to-perfect gap the agent closes.\n\n### 4.2 Primary metric \u2014 Training time (wall clock)\n- **Timed window:** `monotonic_time()` from the first training iteration through the eval that ends the run (budget reached or early stop). Includes eval time (part of the pipeline), broken out separately in logs.\n- **Warmup exclusion:** a short JIT-compile warmup (one mini-batch fwd/bwd + a few env steps) runs before the timed window, and its duration is logged separately. Rationale: first-call JAX compilation is a one-off fixed cost that would otherwise dominate and is not a property of the algorithm.\n- **Time-efficiency curve:** cumulative wall-clock seconds at each 500K-step eval checkpoint (used to build *time-to-threshold*, \u00a75.2).\n- **Claim scope:** timing is **relative/within-machine only** \u2014 comparable across the two algorithms run under the identical protocol on the same cores, explicitly **not** portable to other hardware or to GPU.\n\n### 4.3 Secondary metrics\n- **Sample efficiency:** cumulative env steps to each threshold (env-steps-to-target).\n- **Raw mean episode return** at final eval \u2014 secondary only (per-map shaped rewards; reported but non-comparable across maps).\n- **Non-convergence flags:** if IQL never crosses a low threshold on a map, that threshold column records \"not reached within budget\" rather than an inflated time.\n\n---\n\n## 5. (d) Experimental design, fairness & controls\n\n### 5.1 Controls / fairness contract (confirmed ruling 5)\nBoth algorithms receive:\n- **Same total sampled env steps** per run (10M) and **same eval cadence** (every 500K \u00d7 32 deterministic episodes, same eval episodes seeds) \u2014 so the wall-clock comparison is not polluted by different data consumption or eval frequency.\n- **Identical per-run compute:** each run is its own isolated OS process with a fixed core/thread budget via CPU affinity (e.g., `taskset` + fixed `XLA` thread count), fixed host, no concurrent runs during any timing measurement. Non-timing replicate runs may share the machine on disjoint core groups; timing runs never do.\n- Same seed indices across algorithms (paired design: seed k for PPO uses the same random seed k as IQL, same map) so per-seed differences isolate the algorithm, not seed noise.\n- Same env wrapper, observation/action space, reward scaling, and map config for both algorithms.\n\n### 5.2 Threshold-hitting time curves (circularity-free target)\nBecause the achievable ceiling per map is unknown ahead of time (esp. on hard maps), \"time to a single target\" is pre-registered as **time to cross a fixed ladder of normalized-score thresholds** per map: `T = {0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.8}` (on `2s_vs_1sc`, add `0.9`). Each threshold is a stopping event; the comparison at each threshold is well-defined even if the optimum is unknown, and it naturally exposes non-convergence.\n\n### 5.3 Early stopping & reduced-budget fallback\n- **Early stop:** halt at 50% of budget (5M steps) once normalized score \u2265 0.9 on a map (documented deviation).\n- **Fallback rule (feasibility):** if CPU-only throughput makes full 10M steps prohibitive (see \u00a76), reduce all runs to a uniform 2M (or 5M) steps **for both algorithms across all maps** \u2014 proportionality is preserved because the reduction is applied symmetrically and noted in the report. The eval cadence shrinks proportionally (every 500K \u2192 every 100K).\n\n### 5.4 Optional QMIX arm\nQMIX runs under the exact same budget, eval, seed, and timing protocol, reported as an appendix table (final normalized score and time-to-threshold). It is explicitly non-headline: the core answer is PPO-vs-IQL.\n\n---\n\n## 6. (e) Feasibility & risks\n\n1. **CPU-only throughput (main risk, confirmed ruling 3).** SMAX and JaxMARL algos are written for GPU/TPU vectorisation. On the 224-core CPU-only host, 10M env steps \u00d7 30\u201345 runs could take a long wall-clock time. Mitigation: early-stop; the symmetric reduced-budget fallback (\u00a75.3); pinning fewer cores per run so replications parallelize on disjoint core groups; timing runs isolated. Decision rule: if a single timed run is estimated to exceed the run-hour ceiling, drop to the fallback budget uniformly.\n2. **Environment compatibility at run time.** JaxMARL/SMAX packages are not installable here (network off, Python 3.14.4 present, no ML stack). At execution time the design requires a pinned JaxMARL checkout and a compatible JAX/optax install \u2014 a run-time prerequisite, not a design blocker. Recorded in reproducibility notes.\n3. **Map availability.** Exact SMAX map roster must be verified at run time; substitution rule defined (\u00a73.2).\n4. **Hyperparameter sourcing.** Defaults must be pulled from the JaxMARL repo configs at run time; the provisional table is a placeholder and must be replaced before any run (documented).\n5. **Non-convergence on hard maps (esp. `corridor` for independent IQL).** Possible outcome is IQL failing to cross low thresholds. This is a legitimate, reportable result (not a failure) \u2014 recorded as \"not reached within budget,\" and never imputed or extrapolated.\n6. **Low statistical power (n=5).** Addressed by CI/effect-size reporting rather than binary p-value claims (\u00a77).\n\n---\n\n## 7. (f) Analysis plan\n\n1. **Per (algo, map) aggregation:** across 5 seeds report mean \u00b1 95% CI (and per-seed scatter) of (i) final normalized score at budget, (ii) wall-clock seconds and (iii) env steps to each threshold in `T`. Bootstrap CI on the mean (e.g., 10k resamples) since n is small and distributions are unknown.\n2. **Paired contrasts:** for each map and threshold, the paired difference `time_IQL \u2212 time_PPO` and `finalScore_PPO \u2212 finalScore_IQL` per seed; report mean difference with bootstrap 95% CI on the paired differences. Secondary: two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank / Mann-Whitney, explicitly flagged low-power and not load-bearing.\n3. **Curves:** per-map (a) sample-efficiency curves (x = env steps, y = eval normalized score, mean \u00b1 CI band over seeds) and (b) time-efficiency curves (x = wall-clock seconds, y = eval normalized score). These separate \"wall-clock efficient\" (H1) from \"sample-efficient\" (H2) \u2014 the central fairness nuance.\n4. **Cross-map summary:** unweighted mean of normalized scores across the 3 maps at the fixed 10M-step point and at the fixed time budget, with the caveat that maps differ in difficulty; the per-map table remains the primary view.\n5. **Pre-registered claims to make:** per-map time-to-threshold ranking, per-map final-score ranking, and the joint statement \"at equal 10M env steps PPO/IQL reach X vs Y; PPO/IQL hit threshold t in a vs b wall-clock seconds\" \u2014 with no claim about other hardware or other seeds.\n\n---\n\n## 8. (g) Reproducibility notes\n\n- Pin and record: JaxMARL commit hash, JAX/optax/flax versions, Python version, and a full hyperparameter config table (the \u00a73.3 table, finalized from repo defaults before runs).\n- Per-run log (JSON): seed, map, algo, full config, timestamps (warmup excluded, timed window, per-checkpoint), step counts, eval win rates per checkpoint, raw returns per checkpoint, early-stop flag, and random-baseline values.\n- Hardware snapshot: `lscpu`/`nproc` and CPU-affinity/thread settings recorded for every timing run; timing claim scope (relative/within-machine) restated in the report.\n- Determinism: fixed global seeds per run; eval episodes use fixed episode seeds shared across algorithms per map; JAX on CPU with pinned thread count (document any non-determinism observed, e.g., in reductions).\n- Everything in this section is the protocol and run-time checklist; no experiment was executed as part of producing this design.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Summary of locked decisions (from researcher rulings)\n1. IQL primary Q-Learning arm; QMIX optional one-arm appendix only.\n2. Maps 2s_vs_1sc / 3s_vs_5z / corridor \u2014 verify roster at run time, substitution rule set.\n3. 10M env steps, eval every 500K \u00d7 32 deterministic episodes, early-stop + symmetric reduced-budget fallback.\n4. Normalized score = (WR_agent \u2212 WR_random)/(1 \u2212 WR_random), clipped; WR_random measured on-host (1000 random eps/map); raw returns secondary.\n5. Timing = same env steps, same eval cadence, identical isolated fixed-core processes, JIT warmup excluded & reported; claim is within-machine only.\n6. Deterministic/argmax eval for all final scores.", "evidence": "Approved plan (researcher ruling accepted all six proposed choices verbatim). Inspection evidence: /workspace contains only task.json (which itself notes \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\"); global search found no jaxmarl/s_max/smac material; python 3.14.4 with no ML packages; environment block reports network disabled, gpu_enabled false,